    },
    {
      "heading": "The Multistate Lawsuit Challenging the Rule",
      "body": "On November 23, 2026, a major legal challenge was filed in the U.S. District Court for the District of Massachusetts seeking to invalidate HUD's final rule. The lawsuit is led by New York Attorney General Letitia James and represents a coalition of 22 attorneys general. The plaintiffs include New York and 20 other states plus the District of Columbia, joined by Pennsylvania's governor. The specific plaintiff states identified in the action are Arizona, California, Colorado, Connecticut, Delaware, Hawaii, Illinois, Maine, Maryland, Massachusetts, Michigan, Minnesota, New Jersey, New Mexico, Oregon, Rhode Island, Virginia, Vermont, Washington, Wisconsin, and the District of Columbia, alongside New York.\n\nThe complaint asserts that HUD exceeded its statutory authority under the Housing and Community Development Act of 1980. The states argue that Congress explicitly codified the ability of mixed-status families to receive prorated housing assistance through legislation enacted in 1997, and that HUD lacks the administrative authority to overwrite that statutory compromise by regulation. Furthermore, the coalition argues that HUD violated the Administrative Procedure Act (APA) by issuing an arbitrary and capricious rule that failed to adequately consider the severe harm to tens of thousands of citizen children who could face eviction and homelessness.\n\nThe lawsuit asks the federal court to issue a declaratory judgment that the rule is unlawful, vacate the regulation nationwide, and grant preliminary and permanent injunctive relief preventing HUD from enforcing the policy within the plaintiff jurisdictions. However, tenants and advocates must recognize that as of November 25, 2026, no court order, temporary restraining order, preliminary injunction, or vacatur has been granted. The filing of a lawsuit does not pause a federal regulation. Unless and until a federal judge signs an order blocking the rule, housing providers remain legally bound to implement the December 14, 2026 requirements."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Tenants and Assisted Families Should Do Now",
      "body": "Tenants currently receiving assistance through Public Housing, Housing Choice Vouchers, or Project-based Section 8 should not take drastic actions based on rumor, panic, or unverified social media reports. Families should not surrender their apartments or voluntarily withdraw from voucher programs. The final rule provides specific administrative procedures, notice periods, and extension mechanisms that protect tenants while documentation is gathered.\n\nFirst, contact your local public housing authority or private management office to confirm the exact program governing your unit. As established in the Federal Register, certain programs such as Section 202/811 PRAC are entirely exempt from these requirements. If your unit is subsidized under a covered program, verify which family members have already submitted documentation and which records remain outstanding. If you have previously submitted citizenship documentation, such as a U.S. passport or birth certificate, the rule states that continuous residents only need to satisfy the verification requirement once.\n\nSecond, gather identity and immigration documents immediately for all household members. This includes birth certificates, Social Security cards, naturalization certificates, permanent resident cards, or other eligible immigration records. If obtaining documentation from a government agency takes longer than expected, prepare to submit a formal extension request. Remember that under the regulation, your housing provider must grant an extension of up to 60 days if you submit a written certification that every unverified member holds eligible status and that you are making diligent efforts to retrieve the paperwork.\n\nThird, connect with local legal aid organizations and tenant advocacy groups. Legal aid attorneys can examine your household's specific records, assist with extension requests, and advise you if the federal lawsuit produces an emergency injunction. Finally, if your household budget could be impacted by changes in subsidy calculations or proration, evaluate your spending plan carefully. To evaluate how changes in housing expenses fit alongside other household costs, examine our guidance on [how much to spend on rent](/guides/how-much-should-i-spend-on-rent/) to understand conservative budgeting benchmarks while your administrative paperwork is underway."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Our Takeaway on HUD Verification Requirements",
      "body": "If your household resides in Public Housing, uses a Housing Choice Voucher, or lives in Project-based Section 8 housing, you should treat the December 14, 2026 effective date as an active deadline. Begin assembling proof of citizenship or eligible immigration status for every person listed on your lease immediately. Mixed-status families should not wait for their annual renewal date, because the regulation imposes a strict 90-day submission deadline measured directly from December 14, 2026, which expires on approximately March 14, 2027.\n\nThis recommendation is based on the published regulatory text in Federal Register document 2026-23003 and the current procedural status of the federal docket. Our guidance would change if the U.S. District Court for the District of Massachusetts or another federal court enters an order staying the effective date or granting a preliminary injunction, or if HUD formally issues an administrative stay or revised implementation timeline. Until an official court order or housing authority notice confirms a delay, tenants must assume the verification deadlines remain fully operative."
    }
  ],
